                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "So I came to this after beating the first one, and uhhh it feels like a downgrade across the board.\r\n\r\nThe title screen and menu doesn't work as well as the first one, there is more of a coherent story than the first one, but the gameplay is completely different. \r\n\r\nIt's like if you went from playing Enter the Gungeon, into Valkyria Chronicles. Which while I love both, these graphics and the amount of screen space you have, the slidey movement, and annoying to manage Xcom style percentage to hit just makes this a slog.\r\n\r\nThere's some ambition there, but I honestly would have preferred the gameplay of the first one.\r\n\r\nIt's now structured like a roguelike, where you move through a bunch of points of random events and encounters. Which seem to be almost purely negative, investigate the fruit! Oh it was poisoned from bird boop, take 10 damage to the team. Investigate the sweet aroma! Oh it was a skunk, take 10 damage to the team. Then it's a fight, which just really makes it hard to strategize and balance your health, money and weapons since you can only refill at the store, and you don't get THAT much money, when healing a grunt to full costs you like half your gold.\r\n\r\nThe difficulty seems random as hell too, there's no particular way to tell how much damage you can do or the enemies can do to you. Sometimes you shoot and it does like, 20% and sometimes their head explodes, or you just miss. Similarly when they shoot you they do 1/3 of your health, or like 90%. There isn't much of a learning curve to try and balance their sight cones, your screen space, and the fact that you can shoot like, almost across the whole map at any time but you can only see about 5 character lengths away. \r\n\r\nWhich lends to this weird sequence of moving two feet, use your rifle to scan, then move two feet, use the rifle to scan, rinse & repeat. I like the idea of cover, but since you can get shot while moving too close to the enemies vision cones, and can't see them off screen, it's really hard to judge if it's worth moving to cover, or going further to try and find the enemy.\r\n\r\nIt's just really frustrating and doesn't really operate the way you like... It's very feast or famine. You're exploding heads, or dying accidentally in two shots because you walked around a rock and stepped into a vision cone from off screen. \r\n\r\nThis was enough for me, and not what I was wanting to play so, I just dropped it.",
